WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.700
Hello, it's Ranger Melissa! Happy Glacier Science Day!

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:07.340
We are here in one of my favorite places in Glacier National Park.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:20.240
We are on the trail to Piegan Pass, and not only is this one of my favorite areas, but we are going to learn about one of my favorite alpine critters: the American pika! And in just a

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:30.300
minute, we're going to meet up with biologist and Citizen Science Program Manager, Jami Belt. She's going to tell us all about the pika. Before we do, I just want to tell you that we're

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:43.920
at about 7,000 feet in elevation, and it's about 63 degrees Fahrenheit right now. It's mostly sunny today, a beautiful day actually, to be up here, and around me, you're going to see

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:56.620
lots of different forbs; different wildflowers. Some are yellow, some are white. We have glacier lilies right here, and then there's some subalpine fir, which are pretty short.
